<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> is the region of the African continent centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo basin</a>, including present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and neighbouring countries. It was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> near the Atlantic coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a> in the interior. Both were drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and later colonised by European powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-africa.jpg" alt="Central Africa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sannita · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UN_Macroregion_Central_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> is a subregion whose countries differ according to the definition used. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> geoscheme, which calls it Middle Africa, lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, Equatorial Guinea, Gabon, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and Príncipe. The African Development Bank counts seven countries. The basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> has long been ecologically important to the peoples of the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds go back over 100,000 years, and there is evidence of iron smelting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> that may date to 3000-2500 BCE. Bantu migrants reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region around 2500 BCE and had settled as far south as present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> by the middle of the first millennium BCE. In the north, the Sao civilization lived by the Chari River from about the 6th century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, known by that name from the 9th century CE, gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>, which lasted until 1900.</p><p>In the south, the Bakongo were unified by the 15th century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> under a ruler called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikongo">manikongo</a>, with its capital at M'banza-Kongo. Afonso I, a Christian, took the throne in 1506, and slave trading increased with his wars of conquest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaga">Jaga</a> invaded about 1568-1569, and Manikongo António I's army was destroyed by an Afro-Portuguese army at the Battle of Mbwila in the 1660s, after which the kingdom dissolved into petty polities fighting for war captives to sell. In the 1450s the Luba Ilunga Tshibinda married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lueji">Rweej</a> and united the Lunda peoples; their descendants took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo">Mwata Yamvo</a>, and the empire lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a>, armed with guns, invaded it in the 19th century.</p><p>At the Conference of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1884-85 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was divided among European colonial powers, defining boundaries largely intact today. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, Gabon, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> gained independence in 1960, Equatorial Guinea in 1968, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and Príncipe in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main economic activities are farming, herding and fishing. Bantu languages predominate across much of the region, spoken by peoples such as the Mongo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a>, alongside Nilo-Saharan and Ubangian communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and traditional faiths are the main religions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> is the only country where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is the majority religion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Central Africa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Congo Free State was King Leopold II’s private colony in <b>Central Africa</b>, where forced collection of wild rubber caused mass death and suffering; the international campaign against it became a landmark of human rights activism, and its borders and methods shaped the Belgian Congo and the modern…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the expansionist Jules Ferry the republic acquired Indochina, Madagascar, vast territories in West and <b>Central Africa</b> and much of Polynesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom had fertile agricultural land and access to riverine trade routes, and it lay between the Atlantic coast and the interior of <b>Central Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Luba Empire was one of <b>Central Africa</b>'s most influential states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lunda Empire influenced <b>Central Africa</b> culturally and economically.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese and later Brazilian merchants became its principal organisers, drawing captives above all from West <b>Central Africa</b> through Luanda and other ports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
